# CodeIgniter 4 Standards

> **Note for AI agents**: Read the project's `README.md` first to confirm this is a CI4 project and identify any project-specific overrides. General naming and data type rules are in `rules/coding-standards.mdc` — this file only extends them with CI4-specific conventions.

---

## When to Use CI4

CodeIgniter 4 is used exclusively for **simple commercial websites** with at most one database connection. These projects prioritise speed of development over strict architecture. If business logic grows beyond simple data display, escalate to the development lead for a technology review.

---

## Version and Requirements

- **PHP**: Minimum **PHP 8.2**.
- **CodeIgniter**: Latest stable CI4 version.

---

## Architecture: Simple MVC

CI4 projects follow a straightforward MVC structure. There is **no service layer** — logic lives directly in controllers, as these sites have minimal business logic.

```
app/
├── Controllers/       # One controller per section/feature
├── Models/            # CI4 Models for DB access
├── Views/             # One view per route
├── Config/            # Application configuration
└── Filters/           # Route filters (auth, CSRF, etc.)
```

**One view per route**: each route renders exactly one view file. Keep views clean — no complex logic in PHP view files.

```php
// Controller: simple and direct
class TarifasController extends BaseController
{
    public function index(): string
    {
        $model = new TarifaModel();
        $data  = ['tarifas' => $model->findAll()];
        return view('tarifas/index', $data);
    }
}
```

---

## Database Access

- Use CI4 Models for all DB queries.
- Maximum one database connection per project.
- Never write raw SQL with interpolated user input — always use query builder bindings.

---

## Testing

- Formal testing is **not mandatory** for CI4 projects given their rapid-development focus.
- If tests are written, use CI4's built-in `CIUnitTestCase`.
- Verify manually that the application runs without errors before opening a PR.

---

## Code Quality Checklist Before PR

- [ ] Application runs without errors.
- [ ] Each new route has a corresponding view file.
- [ ] No credentials hardcoded — use environment variables or CI4 config files.
- [ ] No raw SQL with interpolated user input.
